            "presenting_complaint.0",
            "diagnosis_primary.0"
          ],
          "text": "Thank you for referring this 74-year-old male who was admitted with sudden right facial droop, right arm weakness and dysphasia with onset 1 hour prior to admission, with a diagnosis of acute ischaemic stroke.",
          "unverified_fact_ids": []
        },
        {
          "section_id": "sections.12",
          "part": "part_b",
          "heading": "admission_summary",
          "fact_ids": [
            "key_investigations.0",
            "key_investigations.1",
            "key_investigations.2",
            "key_investigations.3",
            "treatment.0",
            "treatment.1",
            "treatment.2",
            "treatment.3"
          ],
          "text": "On admission his NIHSS was 9. CT head on Day 1 showed no haemorrhage, and he received thrombolysis with alteplase within the treatment window. Post-thrombolysis CT on Day 2 confirmed no bleed, and his NIHSS had improved to 4 by Day 2. He was assessed by speech and language therapy (SALT); nasogastric feeding was initiated due to an unsafe swallow and was discontinued after swallow recovery on Day 3. In view of his known atrial fibrillation, anticoagulation with apixaban was commenced per stroke protocol timing on Day 4 and aspirin was stopped. He was accepted by the early supported discharge (ESD) team prior to discharge.",
          "unverified_fact_ids": []
        },
